SymmetricDS keeps a pool of open database connections and lends them to routing, push, pull, load, and other jobs as they run, so that each job does not open a new connection of its own.
Two pool implementations are supported, selected by the `db.pool.type` property in the engine properties file:

* `hikari` - https://github.com/brettwooldridge/HikariCP[HikariCP], the default.
* `dbcp2` - https://commons.apache.org/proper/commons-dbcp/[Apache Commons DBCP2], the default before SymmetricDS 3.18.

Both libraries ship with SymmetricDS, so no additional downloads are needed.

An engine with a separate runtime database (log-based, time-based, or write-only capture) has a second pool for that database.
It is configured with the same properties prefixed by `target.`, for example `target.db.pool.max.active`.

=== Selecting the Pool

Set `db.pool.type` in the engine properties file and restart SymmetricDS.

----
db.pool.type=hikari
----

New installations use HikariCP.
ifdef::pro[]
When the setup program upgrades an installation from a version older than 3.18, it adds `db.pool.type=dbcp2` to every engine properties file that does not already set `db.pool.type`, so an upgraded installation keeps using DBCP2 until you change it.
endif::pro[]
ifndef::pro[]
An installation upgraded from a version older than 3.18 switches to HikariCP unless its engine properties file sets `db.pool.type`. To keep DBCP2, add `db.pool.type=dbcp2` before starting the upgraded installation.
endif::pro[]
When moving an existing deployment from DBCP2 to HikariCP, review the settings under <<Differences Between the Pools>>.

=== Choosing a Pool

HikariCP is recommended for most deployments:

* *Performance* - HikariCP adds very little overhead each time a connection is borrowed and returned.
* *Connection storms* - When many threads need a connection at once, for example after the database comes back from an outage, HikariCP opens new connections one at a time from a background thread and the other threads wait, up to `db.pool.max.wait.millis`. With DBCP2, each waiting thread opens its own connection, so the database can receive a burst of connection attempts at the moment it is recovering.
* *Self-healing* - HikariCP checks each connection before handing it out, pings idle connections every `db.pool.keepalive.time`, and replaces every connection once it reaches `db.pool.max.lifetime.millis`. Connections broken by a database restart, a failover, or a network device are replaced without restarting SymmetricDS. DBCP2 checks a connection when it is borrowed (`db.test.on.borrow`) but does not ping idle connections unless `db.test.while.idle` is enabled, and never retires a connection because of its age.

DBCP2 remains fully supported.
Keep it if your deployment depends on a setting HikariCP does not support, such as several statements in `db.init.sql` or the `db.test.*` validation settings, or if it was tuned and validated on DBCP2 and you are not ready to re-test it.

=== Property Support by Pool

The table shows which pool settings each implementation uses.
See <<Startup Parameters>> for each property's default value and full description.

[cols="2,1,1",options="header"]
|===
|Property |HikariCP |DBCP2

|`db.pool.max.active` |Used |Used
|`db.pool.min.idle` |Used |Used
|`db.pool.max.wait.millis` |Used |Used
|`db.validation.query` |Used if set |Used
|`db.init.sql` |First statement only |All statements
|`db.connection.properties` |Used |Used
|`db.pool.initial.size` |Ignored |Used
|`db.pool.max.idle` |Ignored |Used
|`db.pool.min.evictable.idle.millis` |Ignored |Used
|`db.test.on.borrow` |Ignored |Used
|`db.test.on.return` |Ignored |Used
|`db.test.while.idle` |Ignored |Used
|`db.pool.max.lifetime.millis` |Used |Ignored
|`db.pool.idle.timeout.millis` |Used |Ignored
|`db.pool.keepalive.time` |Used |Ignored
|`db.pool.leak.detection.threshold` |Used |Ignored
|===

=== Differences Between the Pools

Settings carried over from a DBCP2 configuration behave differently, or not at all, under HikariCP:

* `db.pool.max.idle`, `db.pool.initial.size`, `db.pool.min.evictable.idle.millis`, and the `db.test.*` properties are ignored. Their HikariCP counterparts are `db.pool.min.idle`, `db.pool.idle.timeout.millis`, `db.pool.max.lifetime.millis`, and `db.pool.keepalive.time`.
* `db.init.sql` runs only its first statement, and the rest are ignored with a warning. Combine the statements into one, or move session settings into `db.connection.properties` or the JDBC URL where the driver supports it.

`db.pool.max.active` caps the total number of open connections in both pools, so the same value can be used with either one.

=== Tuning HikariCP

The shipped defaults suit most deployments.
Size the pool as described in <<Sizing the Database Connection Pool>> and <<CPU and Memory Sizing>>, and see <<Startup Parameters>> for the recommended range of each setting.
Two settings are worth changing in specific situations:

* *Keepalive* - Set `db.pool.keepalive.time` shorter than the idle timeout of any firewall or load balancer between SymmetricDS and the database, so that idle connections are not dropped.
* *Leak detection* - When troubleshooting a pool that runs out of connections, set `db.pool.leak.detection.threshold` (for example to 60000) to log where long-held connections were borrowed, then set it back to 0.

For further guidance, see HikariCP's https://github.com/brettwooldridge/HikariCP#gear-configuration-knobs-baby[configuration documentation].

=== TCP Keepalive

A network device between SymmetricDS and the database, such as a firewall or load balancer, can silently drop a connection that has been idle for a while.
Neither side is told, so the pool may hand out a connection that no longer works, and a query on it can hang until a socket timeout expires.
`db.pool.keepalive.time` protects connections while they sit idle in the pool, but HikariCP recommends also enabling TCP keepalive in the JDBC driver and the operating system so that the network layer detects dead connections.
See https://github.com/brettwooldridge/HikariCP/wiki/Setting-Driver-or-OS-TCP-Keepalive[Setting Driver or OS TCP Keepalive].

Enable keepalive in the driver with `db.connection.properties` or the JDBC URL:

[cols="1,2",options="header"]
|===
|Database |Driver setting

|PostgreSQL |`tcpKeepAlive=true`
|MySQL |`tcpKeepAlive=true` (enabled by default in Connector/J)
|Oracle |`oracle.net.keepAlive=true`
|===

For example:

----
db.connection.properties=tcpKeepAlive=true
----

The operating system controls how long a connection must be idle before keepalive probes are sent.
The Linux default is two hours, which is longer than many firewalls allow; the HikariCP page above lists recommended operating system settings.
